# Caldera Runner — sculpting and rigging brief

You are sculpting and rigging the Caldera Runner, the fast, fragile chaff of the Slag
— a low, long, ground-hugging obsidian creature that skitters on four legs and lunges
at what it reaches — as a 3D voxel model with a rig a game can pose at runtime. Build
it with `voxel-anim` (painting opaque cells one operation at a time) as a rigged model
that reads unmistakably as this scuttling obsidian thing, runs believably on its legs,
and lunges on demand. There is no target model to copy.

This test measures creativity and craft, not instruction-following. The brief fixes
what the Runner is and how it must move; it deliberately does not hand you a parts
list, joint placements, or pose angles — working out the pieces a running, lunging
creature needs, where they attach, and how they articulate is the test. Invent the rig.

## The volume and coordinate system

- The volume is **{{voxel.width}} wide (x) × {{voxel.height}} tall (y) × {{voxel.depth}} deep (z)**, in opaque voxels. It
  starts empty.
- x runs across the Runner, `0`–`{{voxel.maxX}}`. y runs up, `0` (bottom, the ground) to `{{voxel.maxY}}`
  (top). z runs front-to-back, `0`–`{{voxel.maxZ}}`.
- Forward is +z: the head points toward `z = {{voxel.maxZ}}` (the front) at rest. Up is +y.
- Build the Runner symmetric about the lengthwise vertical centerplane — the four legs
  mirror left/right, and the body, head, and dorsal ridge are centered on it.
- The Runner is deliberately **low and long** — it fills most of the depth, rides only
  a little way off the ground on its legs, and never reads as tall. It is the smallest
  and fastest thing on the field; nothing about it should read as heavy or armored.
- Each part is sculpted separately with `voxel-anim --part <name>`, in this same
  volume's coordinates, positioned where the part sits on the assembled Runner (a leg
  already under its corner, the head already out at the front).

## Palette

Use only these opaque colors:

| Role | Hex |
| --- | --- |
| Body — obsidian | `#241f2b` |
| Deep recess, shadowed seam — obsidian dark | `#16131b` |
| Accent plate — the dorsal ridge (see below) | `#4a4358` |
| Acid glow — the eye slit | `#9ede3a` |

Do **not** use steel `#c9ced6` or violet `#b56bff` anywhere on the model. A game
paints those on at run time (see *The accent region*); baking them in defeats it.

## What the Runner is (and what is yours to invent)

Fixed — the Runner must read unmistakably as all of these:

- A **low, long, wedge-shaped body** of obsidian, narrow at the front and broadening
  slightly toward the back, held close to the ground and running most of the length of
  the volume. Its shadowed seams and recesses are obsidian dark.
- **Four legs**, one at each corner, that carry it and run (see the animations). They
  read as thin, sprung, and quick — the legs of something built for speed, not weight.
- A **narrow, low head** at the front carrying **one single horizontal eye slit** of
  acid glow, and no other glow anywhere on the model. This slit is the Runner's whole
  glow signature: a reviewer must be able to tell the Runner from its siblings by it.
- A **raised dorsal ridge** of plates running along the spine, from behind the head
  toward the back — the accent region below.

The Runner carries **no weapon, no armor plating, and no tools**. It kills by throwing
its body at what it reaches. Its silhouette must not be confused with the heavy,
top-heavy Breaker, the tall spindly Sapper, or the massive Colossus — it is the low,
long, fast one.

Everything else is yours to invent — the exact silhouette and proportions of the body
and head, the number of segments and joints in each leg, how the dorsal ridge is
shaped, and how you break the Runner into rig parts. Nothing here prescribes a shape;
the test rewards a bold, characterful design that is unmistakably the Runner and
animates convincingly.

## The accent region — a hard contract

A game recolors this model at run time to show the Runner's **tier**, by finding every
voxel of one reserved color and repainting it. That reserved color is the **accent
plate** color `#4a4358`, and it is what makes the contract work:

- Sculpt the **dorsal ridge plates** — and only those — in `#4a4358`.
- Use `#4a4358` **nowhere else** on the model. Not a stray voxel, not a highlight, not
  a seam. Every voxel of it is treated as an accent plate.
- The ridge must be **one contiguous run** of plates along the spine, and it must be
  clearly **visible from the side and from above** — that is how a player reads a
  Runner's tier at a glance across the battlefield.

Everything else — the body, the recesses, the legs, the head, the eye slit — keeps its
own color and is never repainted. Get this wrong and every Runner in the game looks the
same tier.

## The required animations — the fixed contract

`rig.json` is pre-seeded with two required animation declarations by name (you author
the motion). Author each with `voxel-anim define-animation` then `add-keyframe`,
choosing the period and each key's `--interp` (`constant`/`linear`/`bezier` or
`ease-in`/`ease-out`/`ease-in-out`, with optional `--in-handle`/`--out-handle`) so the
motion has snap — the Runner is light and quick, so its motion is fast and darting, not
the heavy ease of a big machine.

- **`move`** — the run (a game-triggered playable, `loop = true`). The feet plant on the
  ground and the body skitters over them so it reads as a light creature sprinting, not
  flailing or gliding. Authored in place: the body does not translate across the scene —
  the leg cycle alone carries the stride (played on its own the planted foot slides
  straight back under the body, treadmill-style, then swings forward), and a game
  supplies the real travel. The legs move; the head holds steady and level, eye slit
  forward, so it stays fixed on what it is running at.
- **`attack`** — the lunge (a game-triggered playable, `loop = true`). The Runner coils
  back on its legs, throws its head and body forward in one fast strike, and recoils to
  its stance before looping — a snap out, a recovery. The whole body commits to the
  lunge; this is a creature with no weapon hurling itself at a target. The feet stay
  planted.

You may add your own extra parts, joints, or animations on top of this (for example a
twitch of the ridge plates at rest), but you must not drop or contradict the required
`move` and `attack` animations, by those names.

## Working the tool

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on
your `PATH`. Define your own parts with `define-part` (giving each a parent and a
pivot), sculpt each by selecting it with `--part <name>`, set pivots with `set-pivot`,
place joints with `define-joint`, and author the two animations' keyframes with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Run `voxel-anim --help` for the available operations
(setting and clearing single voxels, filling and stroking boxes, 3D lines, spheres, and
a mirror plane), the rig subcommands, and the animation subcommands, and `voxel-anim
<operation> --help` for each one's exact flags. Call `voxel-anim` once per operation;
run `voxel-anim render` and read `parts/<part>.png` (and the assembled `scene/*.png`
previews) between calls to judge each part against this brief — that each leg's chain
seats under its own corner of the body, the head meets the body, the dorsal ridge runs
unbroken, and the animations read with the right lightness. Run `voxel-anim render`
before you finish so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry your result is built from —
an unrendered part scores as empty (`voxel-anim render --component <part>` renders one
part; `voxel-anim render --time <ms> --animation <name>` renders the model posed at that
instant to check the motion).
